\DOC X86_PROMOTE_RETURN_NOSTACK_TAC

\TYPE {X86_PROMOTE_RETURN_NOSTACK_TAC : thm -> thm -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Given the machine-code definition theorem and an {ensures} theorem about the
stack-free core body, discharges a top-level subroutine-level {ensures x86}
specification stated with a stack pointer and return address.

\DESCRIBE
{mc : thm} is the theorem defining the byte list of the full machine code;
{core : thm} is the already-proved {ensures} specification for the core of the
code (the body without the final return), whose preconditions, postconditions
and {MAYCHANGE} frame are lifted to the subroutine level. The tactic applies to
an assumption-free goal whose conclusion is a universally quantified (possibly
also existentially quantified over an event-trace function) implication from
nonoverlapping/alignment side conditions to an {ensures x86} statement about
the whole code, with {read RSP}/return-address pre- and postconditions and a
{MAYCHANGE [RSP] ,, ...} frame. Normally it closes such a goal outright:
{
         ?- t
   =============================  X86_PROMOTE_RETURN_NOSTACK_TAC mc core
}
\noindent If the postcondition additionally asserts an existential property of
the event trace, one subgoal remains instead: the universal variables (and the
stack/return variables) are specialised, and the side conditions, the
event-trace conclusions of {core}, and facts about the loaded code, the final
{RIP}, {RSP}, event list and the {MAYCHANGE} relation between the intermediate
states are added as assumptions {A}; the remaining conclusion {u} is the
event-trace existential instantiated to the concrete trace extended by the load
and jump events.
{
         ?- t
   =============================  X86_PROMOTE_RETURN_NOSTACK_TAC mc core
         A ?- u
}

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only these two outcomes, on goals of this single
subroutine-specification shape with no initial assumptions.

\ENDDOC
